# Remove roman-numeral entries

## ==Original Issue==

Discovery tokenizes multi-word titles, and roman numerals surface as garbage
entries with wrong pronunciations — e.g. from the current output:
`XV → khʷ`, `VIII → VI.iʲ`, `XII → khee`, `II → ai⁵⁵ai⁵⁵`. These come from
`Louis XIV`, `World War II`, `Chapter VIII`, etc. A TTS app does not need
"pronunciations" for roman numerals.

## Resolution (proposed)

Drop any single-token candidate whose stripped form is entirely roman-numeral
characters — `^[IVXLCDM]{2,}$` (length 2+; the single letters are already
dropped by the single-character rule). Case-insensitive but roman numerals are
normally uppercase in titles; require all-caps to avoid touching words like
"di"/"mix" (those are common nouns and already culled by ticket 16 anyway).

## ==Status Report: 2026-10-24 22:10 US/Pacific==
Implemented. `edgepron/wiktionary/wordify.is_roman_numeral()` matches
`^[IVXLCDM]{2,}$` (uppercase only, so "Li"/"mix" are safe), and
`entries_from_wiktionary` drops such tokens. `XV/VIII/XII/II` etc. are gone from
the output.